# Hack The Box — Connected: Privilege Escalation

## Overview

While compromising the **Connected** machine from Hack The Box, an insecure privilege-escalation path was identified that allowed the `asterisk` user to execute commands as `root`.

The escalation involved an insecure **incrond** configuration combined with a configuration file used by a script executed with root privileges.

> **Note:** This write-up intentionally excludes flags, credentials, IP addresses, hashes, and other sensitive lab information.

## Enumeration

The `incrond` service was first inspected:

```bash
systemctl status incrond
```

The service was active and running with root privileges.

The system-wide `incron` configuration was then examined:

```bash
ls -la /etc/incron.d/
```

The `legacy` configuration contained an interesting rule:

```text
/var/spool/asterisk/sysadmin/dahdi_restart IN_CLOSE_WRITE /usr/sbin/sysadmin_dahdi_restart
```

This meant that a write operation on `dahdi_restart` would trigger the execution of `sysadmin_dahdi_restart`.

## Permission Analysis

The permissions of the trigger directory were checked:

```bash
ls -ld /var/spool/asterisk/sysadmin
```

The directory was writable by the `asterisk` user.

The trigger file was also inspected:

```bash
ls -l /var/spool/asterisk/sysadmin/dahdi_restart
```

The `asterisk` user had sufficient permissions to modify the file and generate the `IN_CLOSE_WRITE` event.

## Identifying the Configuration File

The DAHDI initialization script was examined:

```bash
grep -n 'init.conf' /etc/init.d/dahdi
```

The output showed that `/etc/dahdi/init.conf` was loaded by the script.

Its permissions were also checked:

```bash
ls -l /etc/dahdi/init.conf
```

The file was writable by `asterisk`.

This was significant because the execution chain ultimately involved a process running with root privileges.

## Privileged Execution Test

A controlled test command was added to the configuration to write the output of `id` to a file accessible by the current user.

The trigger was then activated:

```bash
touch /var/spool/asterisk/sysadmin/dahdi_restart
```

After waiting for the event to be processed, the test file contained:

```text
uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)
```

This confirmed that arbitrary commands could be executed with **root privileges**.

## Privilege Escalation Chain

The complete attack path can be summarized as:

```text
asterisk
   │
   ├── can modify /etc/dahdi/init.conf
   │
   ├── can modify dahdi_restart
   │
   ▼
incrond (root)
   │
   ▼
sysadmin_dahdi_restart
   │
   ▼
loads attacker-controlled configuration
   │
   ▼
command execution
   │
   ▼
root
```

## Obtaining Root Privileges

Once root-level command execution had been confirmed, the same execution path was used to perform a controlled action on a file accessible to the `asterisk` user.

This confirmed that the root flag could be accessed without including its actual contents in this write-up.

## Conclusion

The privilege escalation was possible due to the combination of:

* An `incrond` service running as `root`.
* An `incron` rule executing a privileged script.
* A trigger file writable by `asterisk`.
* A configuration file used during the privileged execution that was also writable by `asterisk`.

Together, these conditions allowed the `asterisk` user to turn a seemingly limited file-write capability into **arbitrary command execution as root**.

